WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:03.556 --> 00:06.386 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference June 12,

00:06.566 --> 00:07.856 align:middle
2024 CHAIR POWELL.

00:08.386 --> 00:09.276 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:09.686 --> 00:12.046 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:12.046 --> 00:15.616 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:15.616 --> 00:18.246 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:18.766 --> 00:21.926 align:middle
Our economy has made
considerable progress toward

00:21.926 --> 00:23.966 align:middle
both goals over the
past two years.

00:24.876 --> 00:26.876 align:middle
The labor market has
come into better balance,

00:27.456 --> 00:30.636 align:middle
with continued strong job gains
and a low unemployment rate.

00:31.916 --> 00:34.086 align:middle
Inflation has eased
substantially from a peak

00:34.086 --> 00:37.956 align:middle
of 7 percent to 2.7 percent
but is still too high.

00:39.236 --> 00:41.316 align:middle
We are strongly committed
to returning inflation

00:41.316 --> 00:44.226 align:middle
to our 2 percent goal in
support of a strong economy

00:44.316 --> 00:45.626 align:middle
that benefits everyone.

00:47.466 --> 00:51.186 align:middle
Today, the FOMC decided to
leave our policy interest rate

00:51.326 --> 00:54.786 align:middle
unchanged and to continue to
reduce our securities holdings.

00:56.306 --> 00:57.956 align:middle
We are maintaining
our restrictive stance

00:57.956 --> 01:01.736 align:middle
of monetary policy in order to
keep demand in line with supply

01:01.976 --> 01:03.866 align:middle
and reduce inflationary
pressures.

01:05.026 --> 01:06.996 align:middle
I'll have more to say
about monetary policy

01:06.996 --> 01:09.786 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:10.996 --> 01:14.116 align:middle
Recent indicators suggest that
economic activity has continued

01:14.116 --> 01:15.906 align:middle
to expand at a solid pace.

01:16.896 --> 01:20.136 align:middle
Although GDP growth
moderated from 3.4 percent

01:20.136 --> 01:23.396 align:middle
in the fourth quarter of
last year to 1.3 percent

01:23.396 --> 01:27.216 align:middle
in the first quarter, private
domestic final purchases-which

01:27.216 --> 01:30.166 align:middle
excludes inventory
investment, government spending,

01:30.166 --> 01:33.296 align:middle
and net exports and usually
sends a clearer signal

01:33.296 --> 01:37.236 align:middle
on underlying demand-grew at 2.8
percent in the first quarter,

01:37.526 --> 01:40.416 align:middle
nearly as strong as the
second half of 2023.

01:41.736 --> 01:43.516 align:middle
Growth of consumer
spending has slowed

01:43.516 --> 01:46.476 align:middle
from last year's robust
pace but remains solid.

01:47.406 --> 01:50.306 align:middle
And investment in equipment
and intangibles has picked

01:50.306 --> 01:52.546 align:middle
up from its anemic
pace last year.

01:54.036 --> 01:56.866 align:middle
Improving supply conditions
have supported resilient demand

01:57.096 --> 01:58.196 align:middle
and the strong performance

01:58.196 --> 02:00.146 align:middle
of the U.S. economy
over the past year.

02:01.596 --> 02:03.366 align:middle
In our Summary of
Economic Projections,

02:03.436 --> 02:06.796 align:middle
Committee participants generally
expect GDP growth to slow

02:06.796 --> 02:09.426 align:middle
from last year's pace,
with a median projection

02:09.426 --> 02:14.246 align:middle
of 2.1 percent this year and 2.0
percent over the next two years.

02:16.196 --> 02:17.306 align:middle
In the labor market, supply

02:17.306 --> 02:19.626 align:middle
and demand conditions have
come into better balance.

02:20.366 --> 02:24.736 align:middle
Payroll job gains averaged
218,000 jobs per month in April

02:24.736 --> 02:27.886 align:middle
and May, a pace that is
still strong but a bit below

02:27.886 --> 02:29.256 align:middle
that seen in the first quarter.

02:30.436 --> 02:33.646 align:middle
The unemployment rate ticked up
but remains low at 4 percent.

02:34.896 --> 02:37.076 align:middle
Strong job creation
over the past couple

02:37.076 --> 02:40.146 align:middle
of years has been accompanied
by an increase in the supply

02:40.146 --> 02:42.306 align:middle
of workers, reflecting increases

02:42.306 --> 02:46.646 align:middle
in participation among
individuals aged 25 to 54 years

02:47.106 --> 02:49.286 align:middle
and a continued strong
pace of immigration.

02:51.016 --> 02:53.486 align:middle
Nominal wage growth has
eased over the past year,

02:53.646 --> 02:55.716 align:middle
and the jobs-to-workers
gap has narrowed.

02:56.456 --> 02:58.796 align:middle
Overall, a broad set
of indicators suggests

02:58.796 --> 03:01.266 align:middle
that conditions in the labor
market have returned to about

03:01.266 --> 03:03.206 align:middle
where they stood on the eve

03:03.206 --> 03:06.476 align:middle
of the pandemic-relatively
tight but not overheated.

03:08.156 --> 03:11.776 align:middle
FOMC participants expect labor
market strength to continue.

03:12.326 --> 03:14.076 align:middle
The median unemployment
rate projection

03:14.076 --> 03:19.186 align:middle
in the SEP is 4.0 percent at the
end of this year and 4.2 percent

03:19.186 --> 03:20.126 align:middle
at the end of next year.

03:21.526 --> 03:24.576 align:middle
Inflation has eased notably over
the past two years but remains

03:24.576 --> 03:26.896 align:middle
above our longer-run
goal of 2 percent.

03:27.866 --> 03:30.436 align:middle
Total PCE prices
rose 2.7 percent

03:30.466 --> 03:32.136 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in April;

03:32.826 --> 03:35.166 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:35.576 --> 03:38.006 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.8 percent.

03:39.086 --> 03:42.886 align:middle
The consumer price index-which
came out this morning and tends

03:42.886 --> 03:47.856 align:middle
to run higher than the PCE, PCE
price index-rose 3.3 percent

03:47.996 --> 03:49.516 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in May,

03:50.086 --> 03:52.496 align:middle
and the core CPI
rose 3.4 percent.

03:53.826 --> 03:56.476 align:middle
The inflation data received
earlier this year were higher

03:56.476 --> 03:58.836 align:middle
than expected, though more
recent monthly readings have

03:58.836 --> 03:59.646 align:middle
eased somewhat.

04:00.886 --> 04:02.726 align:middle
Longer-term inflation
expectations appear

04:02.726 --> 04:05.716 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

04:05.716 --> 04:09.136 align:middle
of surveys of households and
businesses and forecasters,

04:09.136 --> 04:11.056 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

04:12.286 --> 04:13.926 align:middle
The median projection in the SEP

04:13.926 --> 04:17.356 align:middle
for total PCE inflation
is 2.6 percent this year,

04:17.956 --> 04:22.666 align:middle
2.3 percent next year,
and 2.0 percent in 2026.

04:24.566 --> 04:26.266 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

04:26.436 --> 04:29.386 align:middle
that high inflation imposes
significant hardship,

04:29.616 --> 04:32.996 align:middle
as it erodes purchasing power,
especially for those least able

04:32.996 --> 04:35.516 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

04:35.706 --> 04:37.046 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

04:37.656 --> 04:41.346 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

04:41.346 --> 04:43.606 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

04:43.946 --> 04:44.946 align:middle
for the American people.

04:45.926 --> 04:47.976 align:middle
In support of these goals,
the Committee decided

04:47.976 --> 04:50.096 align:middle
at today's meeting to
maintain the target range

04:50.096 --> 04:53.246 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate at 5¼ to 5½ percent

04:53.796 --> 04:55.976 align:middle
and to continue reducing
our securities holdings.

04:56.956 --> 05:00.026 align:middle
As labor market tightness has
eased and inflation has declined

05:00.026 --> 05:03.096 align:middle
over the past year, the risks
to achieving our employment

05:03.166 --> 05:06.126 align:middle
and inflation goals have
moved toward better balance.

05:07.316 --> 05:09.126 align:middle
The economic outlook
is uncertain, however,

05:09.346 --> 05:12.106 align:middle
and we remain highly
attentive to inflation risks.

05:13.686 --> 05:15.946 align:middle
We've stated that we do not
expect it will be appropriate

05:15.946 --> 05:18.236 align:middle
to reduce the target range
for the federal funds rate

05:18.696 --> 05:20.546 align:middle
until we have gained
greater confidence

05:20.576 --> 05:23.276 align:middle
that inflation is moving
sustainably toward 2 percent.

05:24.226 --> 05:26.696 align:middle
So far this year, the
data have not given us

05:26.856 --> 05:27.856 align:middle
that greater confidence.

05:28.736 --> 05:30.666 align:middle
The most recent inflation
readings have been more

05:30.666 --> 05:32.596 align:middle
favorable than earlier
in the year, however,

05:32.946 --> 05:35.566 align:middle
and there has been modest
further progress toward our

05:35.566 --> 05:36.576 align:middle
inflation objective.

05:37.266 --> 05:40.626 align:middle
We will need to see more good
data to bolster our confidence

05:40.676 --> 05:43.346 align:middle
that inflation is moving
sustainably toward 2 percent.

05:44.936 --> 05:47.466 align:middle
We know that reducing
policy restraint too soon

05:47.796 --> 05:50.756 align:middle
or too much could result in
a reversal of the progress

05:50.796 --> 05:52.186 align:middle
that we've seen on inflation.

05:53.186 --> 05:56.026 align:middle
At the same time, reducing
policy restraint too late

05:56.026 --> 05:59.196 align:middle
or too little could unduly
weaken economic activity

05:59.196 --> 05:59.876 align:middle
and employment.

06:01.296 --> 06:03.706 align:middle
In considering any
adjustments to the target range

06:03.706 --> 06:04.676 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,

06:05.106 --> 06:07.416 align:middle
the Committee will carefully
assess incoming data,

06:07.906 --> 06:10.626 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

06:10.936 --> 06:15.586 align:middle
In our SEP, FOMC
participants wrote

06:15.586 --> 06:17.356 align:middle
down their individual
assessments

06:17.416 --> 06:19.546 align:middle
of an appropriate path for
the federal funds rate,

06:20.216 --> 06:22.146 align:middle
based on what each
participant judges

06:22.146 --> 06:24.426 align:middle
to be the most likely
scenario going forward.

06:25.386 --> 06:27.086 align:middle
If the economy evolves
as expected,

06:27.236 --> 06:30.146 align:middle
the median participant projects
that the appropriate level

06:30.146 --> 06:32.726 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
will be 5.1 percent at the end

06:32.726 --> 06:36.236 align:middle
of this year, 4.1 percent
at the end of 2025,

06:36.736 --> 06:39.636 align:middle
and 3.1 percent at
the end of 2026.

06:40.976 --> 06:43.016 align:middle
But these projections
are not a Committee plan

06:43.016 --> 06:44.276 align:middle
or any kind of a decision.

06:44.646 --> 06:45.846 align:middle
As the economy evolves,

06:45.916 --> 06:49.586 align:middle
assessments of the appropriate
policy, policy path will adjust

06:49.616 --> 06:51.786 align:middle
in order to best promote
our maximum-employment

06:51.836 --> 06:53.246 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

06:54.316 --> 06:56.756 align:middle
If the economy remains solid
and inflation persists,

06:57.076 --> 06:59.176 align:middle
we're prepared to maintain
the current target range

06:59.176 --> 07:01.326 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
as long as appropriate.

07:02.236 --> 07:04.326 align:middle
If the labor market were
to weaken unexpectedly

07:04.436 --> 07:06.996 align:middle
or if inflation were to fall
more quickly than anticipated,

07:07.376 --> 07:08.386 align:middle
we're prepared to respond.

07:09.236 --> 07:11.676 align:middle
Policy is well positioned
to deal with the risks

07:11.676 --> 07:12.886 align:middle
and uncertainties that we face

07:13.216 --> 07:15.416 align:middle
in pursuing both sides
of our dual mandate.

07:16.306 --> 07:18.416 align:middle
We'll continue to make our
decisions meeting by meeting,

07:18.856 --> 07:22.046 align:middle
based on the totality of the
data and its implications

07:22.106 --> 07:23.836 align:middle
for the outlook and
the balance of risks.

07:25.656 --> 07:27.136 align:middle
The Fed has been
assigned two goals

07:27.136 --> 07:30.326 align:middle
for monetary policy-maximum
employment and stable prices.

07:30.616 --> 07:32.896 align:middle
We remain committed to
bringing inflation back

07:32.896 --> 07:34.306 align:middle
down to our 2 percent goal

07:34.306 --> 07:36.696 align:middle
and to keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

07:37.656 --> 07:38.336 align:middle
well anchored.

07:39.226 --> 07:41.656 align:middle
Restoring price stability
is essential

07:41.776 --> 07:43.226 align:middle
to achieving maximum employment

07:43.296 --> 07:45.186 align:middle
and stable prices
over the long run.

07:46.176 --> 07:47.256 align:middle
Our success in delivering

07:47.256 --> 07:49.376 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

07:49.896 --> 07:52.766 align:middle
We understand that our actions
affect communities, families,

07:52.766 --> 07:54.176 align:middle
and businesses across
the country.

07:55.096 --> 07:57.486 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:58.256 --> 07:59.866 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

08:00.076 --> 08:01.886 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

08:01.886 --> 08:03.286 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

08:04.136 --> 08:05.956 align:middle
Thank you, and I look
forward to your questions.

08:06.526 --> 08:07.436 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:07.726 --> 08:08.596 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

08:10.646 --> 08:11.966 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

08:12.166 --> 08:13.466 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

08:14.156 --> 08:15.976 align:middle
Just wondering if
you could walk me

08:16.096 --> 08:21.776 align:middle
through the Committee's average
inflation forecast-core PCE is

08:21.776 --> 08:25.026 align:middle
now forecast to be 2.8
percent by the year-end.

08:25.896 --> 08:29.806 align:middle
It's already 2.75, and
after today's number,

08:29.806 --> 08:33.116 align:middle
there were several forecasts on
the street that it would be 2.6

08:33.116 --> 08:34.066 align:middle
at the end of this month.

08:34.306 --> 08:38.396 align:middle
Does that tell you that the
average official expects no

08:38.456 --> 08:40.626 align:middle
further progress in
inflation and, in fact,

08:40.686 --> 08:42.196 align:middle
that it's going to get worse?

08:42.826 --> 08:45.136 align:middle
And if you have this
wrong, doesn't it mean

08:45.136 --> 08:48.436 align:middle
that you sort of-you, you
could have wrong the outlook

08:48.436 --> 08:49.066 align:middle
for rates there?

08:49.286 --> 08:49.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:50.106 --> 08:55.046 align:middle
Yeah. So what's going on there
is that we had very low readings

08:55.636 --> 08:57.386 align:middle
in the second half
of last year-June

08:57.386 --> 08:58.466 align:middle
through December, really.

08:58.916 --> 09:00.066 align:middle
And we're now lapping those.

09:00.066 --> 09:01.886 align:middle
So as you go through
the 12-month window,

09:02.396 --> 09:04.956 align:middle
a very low reading drops out,
and a new reading comes in.

09:04.956 --> 09:07.286 align:middle
The new reading gets added
to the 12-month window.

09:07.696 --> 09:11.206 align:middle
So it's just a, a slight element
of conservativism that we're,

09:11.206 --> 09:16.716 align:middle
we're assuming a certain
level of-of, you know,

09:16.716 --> 09:20.386 align:middle
incoming monthly PCE
and core PCE numbers.

09:20.386 --> 09:23.566 align:middle
We're assuming, you know,
good, but not great, numbers.

09:23.566 --> 09:25.456 align:middle
And if you put that on
top of where we are now,

09:25.766 --> 09:27.426 align:middle
you get a very slight increase

09:27.546 --> 09:31.966 align:middle
in the 12-month-in the
12-month, you know, reading.

09:32.196 --> 09:34.116 align:middle
Now, do we have high
confidence that that's right?

09:34.196 --> 09:35.896 align:middle
No. It's just the kind
of conservative way

09:35.896 --> 09:36.936 align:middle
for forecasting things.

09:37.226 --> 09:40.016 align:middle
If we were to get more readings
like today's reading, then,

09:40.016 --> 09:41.346 align:middle
of course, that wouldn't
be the case.

09:41.696 --> 09:43.486 align:middle
So it's just a forecasting
device.

09:43.486 --> 09:46.906 align:middle
I think, I think-let me say
that we welcome today's reading

09:47.066 --> 09:48.376 align:middle
and then hope for
more like that.

09:48.376 --> 09:48.706 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

09:48.826 --> 09:50.636 align:middle
But if it comes in-just
to follow up-if it comes

09:50.636 --> 09:54.086 align:middle
in the way you forecast it,
it would seem strange for you

09:54.086 --> 09:57.866 align:middle
to be cutting rates at all in
context of a rising core PCE.

09:57.906 --> 09:58.266 align:middle
Thank you.

09:59.846 --> 10:00.096 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:00.096 --> 10:02.096 align:middle
Yeah-no. I mean, I think
we've, we've-what we said is

10:02.096 --> 10:05.676 align:middle
that we don't think it'll be
appropriate to, to reduce rates

10:05.676 --> 10:08.826 align:middle
and begin to loosen policy
until we have more confidence

10:08.826 --> 10:10.746 align:middle
that inflation is moving
back down to 2 percent

10:10.786 --> 10:13.696 align:middle
on a-on a sustainable basis.

10:14.186 --> 10:16.246 align:middle
And that's the-that's
the test we've applied.

10:16.246 --> 10:18.886 align:middle
I don't know that-I
think if we-I don't know

10:18.886 --> 10:20.236 align:middle
that this rules that in or out.

10:20.236 --> 10:20.876 align:middle
I mean, really, it's,

10:20.876 --> 10:23.426 align:middle
it's a forecast-a fairly
conservative forecast month

10:23.486 --> 10:26.686 align:middle
by month that would lead to
slightly higher, you know,

10:26.906 --> 10:29.996 align:middle
12-months rates by
the end of the year.

10:30.046 --> 10:33.826 align:middle
If we get, you know,
good-better readings than that,

10:33.826 --> 10:35.946 align:middle
then you will see that come
down or, or remain the same.

10:36.196 --> 10:39.016 align:middle
If you're at 2.6, 2.7
[percent], you know, that's,

10:39.016 --> 10:40.136 align:middle
that's a really good
place to be.

10:40.136 --> 10:42.176 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:43.746 --> 10:45.096 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

10:45.356 --> 10:47.976 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

10:48.056 --> 10:50.646 align:middle
Chair Powell, if I look at
the, the rate projections,

10:50.646 --> 10:55.466 align:middle
I see 15 of the 19 that
are anticipating either one

10:55.466 --> 10:58.426 align:middle
or two cut-two cuts this
year-fairly evenly split

10:58.426 --> 10:59.146 align:middle
between the two.

10:59.856 --> 11:03.716 align:middle
And so I wonder if you could
explain a little more the

11:03.796 --> 11:05.526 align:middle
nuances or the differences
there.

11:05.896 --> 11:09.636 align:middle
Would two or three more
inflation readings like the one

11:09.956 --> 11:13.526 align:middle
that we saw this morning make
a September interest rate

11:13.576 --> 11:14.426 align:middle
cut possible?

11:14.426 --> 11:17.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:17.316 --> 11:21.696 align:middle
So as far as the SEP part of
that is concerned-as you know,

11:21.696 --> 11:24.476 align:middle
I talk to-I talk to all
of the other participants

11:24.476 --> 11:27.536 align:middle
on the FOMC every cycle, and
we talk about their Summary

11:27.536 --> 11:29.996 align:middle
of Economic Projections and,
and the dot plot-and, you know,

11:29.996 --> 11:31.066 align:middle
their dot plot and everything.

11:31.506 --> 11:35.366 align:middle
And what you-what I hear and see
is that people are looking at,

11:35.366 --> 11:37.626 align:middle
at, you know, a range
of plausible outcomes.

11:37.626 --> 11:40.336 align:middle
And in many cases,
they're-they're thinking,

11:40.336 --> 11:41.166 align:middle
"I don't really"-you know,

11:41.166 --> 11:42.936 align:middle
"I can't really distinguish
between two of these.

11:42.936 --> 11:44.036 align:middle
They're so close for me."

11:44.036 --> 11:46.016 align:middle
These are very close calls,
but we ask them to write

11:46.016 --> 11:48.486 align:middle
down the most likely
one-so they do.

11:48.856 --> 11:50.976 align:middle
And as, I think, you've,
you've-as you've said,

11:50.976 --> 11:52.496 align:middle
there's 15 of the 19 are,

11:52.496 --> 11:55.736 align:middle
are kind of clustered
around one or two.

11:55.736 --> 11:59.296 align:middle
So I think I would look at,
at all-I'd look at all of them

11:59.296 --> 12:03.346 align:middle
as plausible, but I'd look at-so
I think that does tell you kind

12:03.346 --> 12:04.146 align:middle
of what the Committee thinks.

12:04.146 --> 12:07.186 align:middle
But what everyone agrees on is,
it's going to be data dependent.

12:07.696 --> 12:11.096 align:middle
No one-no one brings to this
or takes away from it that is

12:11.096 --> 12:13.516 align:middle
on the Committee a
really strong commitment

12:13.516 --> 12:14.796 align:middle
to a particular rate path.

12:14.796 --> 12:17.176 align:middle
It's actually just
their forecast

12:17.176 --> 12:19.376 align:middle
and their-it's a
combination of their forecast

12:19.376 --> 12:21.216 align:middle
and their own reaction function.

12:21.726 --> 12:24.176 align:middle
But, again, everyone would say

12:24.426 --> 12:25.946 align:middle
that this is very
data dependent,

12:25.946 --> 12:27.626 align:middle
and I don't hold it
with high confidence.

12:27.626 --> 12:31.386 align:middle
And I think if you're looking
for-and, and don't really think

12:31.386 --> 12:34.186 align:middle
that they're-they're not trying
to send a strong signal that

12:34.246 --> 12:35.726 align:middle
"this is what I think
is the right thing."

12:35.726 --> 12:37.646 align:middle
It's just what they
think at a given moment

12:37.646 --> 12:39.536 align:middle
in time, subject to data.

12:39.926 --> 12:42.396 align:middle
In terms of, you know,
future meetings, we haven't,

12:42.826 --> 12:45.686 align:middle
you know-we don't-we don't
try to-we don't make decisions

12:45.686 --> 12:47.216 align:middle
about future meetings
until we get there.

12:47.576 --> 12:49.186 align:middle
I think in terms of
what we need to see,

12:49.636 --> 12:52.076 align:middle
I mentioned it earlier-we,
we want to-we want

12:52.076 --> 12:53.476 align:middle
to gain further confidence.

12:53.476 --> 12:56.766 align:middle
Certainly, more good inflation
readings will help with that.

12:57.066 --> 12:59.476 align:middle
I'm not going to be specific
about how many because,

12:59.806 --> 13:00.596 align:middle
you know, really, it's going

13:00.596 --> 13:02.996 align:middle
to be not just the inflation
rating-readings-it's going

13:02.996 --> 13:05.246 align:middle
to be the totality of the
data: what's happening

13:05.246 --> 13:06.926 align:middle
in the labor market, what's
happening with the balance

13:06.926 --> 13:09.426 align:middle
of risks, what's
happening with the forecast,

13:09.426 --> 13:10.306 align:middle
what's happening with growth.

13:10.306 --> 13:14.076 align:middle
You look at all of that, and
you ask, "Are we confident?

13:14.076 --> 13:16.046 align:middle
Have we reached an
appropriate level of confidence

13:16.116 --> 13:18.846 align:middle
that inflation is moving down
sustainably to 2 percent, or,

13:18.846 --> 13:22.566 align:middle
alternatively, do we see really
unexpected signs of weakness

13:22.566 --> 13:23.756 align:middle
in the labor market
that would call

13:23.756 --> 13:26.146 align:middle
for a response?"-which
is-which is another thing

13:26.146 --> 13:27.416 align:middle
that could, could happen.

13:27.416 --> 13:28.926 align:middle
But, again, we don't see that.

13:29.426 --> 13:34.846 align:middle
And I-we do see today's-we see
today's report as, as progress

13:34.846 --> 13:36.616 align:middle
and as, you know,
building confidence.

13:36.616 --> 13:39.296 align:middle
But we, we don't see ourselves
as having the confidence

13:39.296 --> 13:43.456 align:middle
that would warrant-you know,
that would warrant beginning to,

13:43.816 --> 13:45.846 align:middle
to loosen policy at this time.

13:45.846 --> 13:46.616 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

13:46.616 --> 13:48.666 align:middle
And if I could quickly
follow up-did, did you or any

13:48.666 --> 13:53.016 align:middle
of your colleagues change
your interest rate projections

13:53.146 --> 13:56.986 align:middle
after 8:30 or whenever you got
the inflation numbers today?

13:57.326 --> 13:57.796 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:57.796 --> 14:01.236 align:middle
So we-this happens too often,
but it, it does happen.

14:01.236 --> 14:04.166 align:middle
It just is, you know-data came-I
think it happened a couple

14:04.166 --> 14:05.686 align:middle
of meetings ago-a
few meetings ago.

14:06.126 --> 14:09.046 align:middle
So when that happens-when
there's an important data print

14:09.046 --> 14:12.586 align:middle
during the meeting, first day
or second day-what we do is, we,

14:12.696 --> 14:14.436 align:middle
we make sure people remember

14:14.436 --> 14:16.566 align:middle
that they have the
ability to update.

14:16.666 --> 14:17.746 align:middle
We tell them how to do that.

14:18.436 --> 14:20.856 align:middle
And, and some people
do; some people don't.

14:20.856 --> 14:21.986 align:middle
Most, most people don't.

14:22.516 --> 14:24.666 align:middle
But I'm not going to
get into the specifics,

14:24.666 --> 14:25.986 align:middle
but you have the
ability to do that.

14:25.986 --> 14:27.006 align:middle
So that, you know-what's

14:27.006 --> 14:30.626 align:middle
in the SEP actually does reflect
the data that we got today

14:30.626 --> 14:33.296 align:middle
to the extent you can, you
know, reflect it in one day.

14:33.856 --> 14:36.486 align:middle
I think we'll, you know-you
will see PPI tomorrow.

14:36.486 --> 14:38.966 align:middle
We'll know more about the
PCE [inflation] reading

14:38.966 --> 14:39.976 align:middle
as the month goes on.

14:40.016 --> 14:42.806 align:middle
But the initial CPI
reading and its, you know,

14:42.806 --> 14:44.876 align:middle
kind of first-level
translation to,

14:45.116 --> 14:47.186 align:middle
to PCE [inflation] we
did have this morning.

14:47.186 --> 14:49.516 align:middle
We were briefed about
it, and people were able

14:49.516 --> 14:51.596 align:middle
to consider whether they
should make changes.

14:51.596 --> 14:55.026 align:middle
And, as I said, you know,
some people generally do,

14:55.026 --> 14:56.206 align:middle
but most people generally don't.

14:58.046 --> 14:58.506 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:59.206 --> 15:02.946 align:middle
Jonnelle. JONNELLE MARTE.

15:03.746 --> 15:04.476 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

15:04.476 --> 15:05.836 align:middle
Jonnelle Marte from Bloomberg.

15:05.976 --> 15:09.016 align:middle
As you noted, the labor market
is now in many ways back

15:09.016 --> 15:11.266 align:middle
to where it was before
the pandemic.

15:11.266 --> 15:12.426 align:middle
I wondered if you could comment

15:12.426 --> 15:14.566 align:middle
on how officials
are viewing that.

15:14.626 --> 15:17.896 align:middle
So do you think that there
still needs to be more cooling

15:17.896 --> 15:19.806 align:middle
in the labor market to
bring inflation all the way

15:19.806 --> 15:22.406 align:middle
down to 2 percent,
or is there any sense

15:22.406 --> 15:24.806 align:middle
that maybe the labor market
is more vulnerable now

15:24.806 --> 15:28.276 align:middle
to higher rates now that many
of those imbalances have eased?

15:30.546 --> 15:31.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:31.436 --> 15:36.396 align:middle
Sure. So by, by so many
measures, the labor market was,

15:36.396 --> 15:38.186 align:middle
was kind of overheated
two years ago.

15:38.616 --> 15:41.696 align:middle
And we've seen it gradually move
back into much better balance

15:41.696 --> 15:43.046 align:middle
between supply and demand.

15:43.446 --> 15:44.306 align:middle
So, what have we seen?

15:44.876 --> 15:48.096 align:middle
We've seen labor force
supply come up quite a bit

15:48.296 --> 15:50.676 align:middle
through immigration and through
recovering participation.

15:51.286 --> 15:55.066 align:middle
That's ongoing, mostly now
through the immigration channel.

15:55.066 --> 15:56.906 align:middle
But still, we've
had some increases

15:56.906 --> 15:58.666 align:middle
in prime-age labor force.

15:59.346 --> 16:03.806 align:middle
In terms of on the demand side,
you know, we've seen-well,

16:03.916 --> 16:06.366 align:middle
we've seen quits moving down.

16:06.656 --> 16:09.286 align:middle
We've seen job openings
moving down.

16:09.286 --> 16:12.406 align:middle
We've seen wage increases
moving from very,

16:12.406 --> 16:14.456 align:middle
very high levels a
couple of years ago back

16:14.456 --> 16:15.786 align:middle
down to more sustainable levels.

16:16.416 --> 16:19.686 align:middle
We have seen unemployment
creep up now sixth-tenths

16:19.776 --> 16:22.386 align:middle
over the course of, of a year
or so very, very gradually.

16:22.866 --> 16:23.486 align:middle
So you put all

16:23.486 --> 16:26.566 align:middle
that together-what you have
is still-low unemployment,

16:26.566 --> 16:28.666 align:middle
still 4 percent
unemployment-historically low.

16:29.226 --> 16:31.606 align:middle
But it's moved up a little
bit-it's softened a bit-and,

16:31.606 --> 16:33.316 align:middle
and, you know, that's
an important statistic.

16:33.576 --> 16:35.996 align:middle
But, but more than that,
you've got strong job creation.

16:36.196 --> 16:39.496 align:middle
You, you have payroll jobs still
coming in strong, even though,

16:39.686 --> 16:40.806 align:middle
you know, there's an argument

16:40.806 --> 16:43.266 align:middle
that they may be a bit
overstated but still they're,

16:43.266 --> 16:44.136 align:middle
they're still-they're strong.

16:44.646 --> 16:45.736 align:middle
So that's what we see.

16:45.736 --> 16:48.406 align:middle
We, we watch the labor market,
of course, and the economy

16:48.546 --> 16:51.846 align:middle
as a whole-but the labor market
very carefully-and that's what

16:51.846 --> 16:52.166 align:middle
we see.

16:52.166 --> 16:55.976 align:middle
We see gradual cooling-gradual
moving toward better balance.

16:56.416 --> 16:59.796 align:middle
We're monitoring it carefully
for signs of, of something more

16:59.796 --> 17:01.116 align:middle
than that, but we
really don't see that.

17:01.116 --> 17:01.886 align:middle
JONNELLE MARTE.

17:01.886 --> 17:04.266 align:middle
As a quick follow-up-the
surveys that make

17:04.266 --> 17:06.716 align:middle
up the jobs report are, are
showing different tales.

17:06.716 --> 17:09.476 align:middle
There's been some
divergence, especially, we saw,

17:09.476 --> 17:11.316 align:middle
in the last report
that we got on Friday.

17:11.356 --> 17:14.116 align:middle
So how do you interpret
that, and,

17:14.316 --> 17:16.486 align:middle
and how does it change your
view on the labor market?

17:16.486 --> 17:17.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:17.026 --> 17:19.936 align:middle
So sometimes it's-you can't
reconcile the differences.

17:19.936 --> 17:21.596 align:middle
You just have to, have
to-have to look at it and,

17:21.596 --> 17:22.726 align:middle
and try to understand.

17:22.726 --> 17:25.886 align:middle
And that's why it always makes
sense to look at a series,

17:25.886 --> 17:28.026 align:middle
you know, in 6-in
3, 6, and 12 months

17:28.026 --> 17:29.426 align:middle
of things rather
than just one report.

17:29.426 --> 17:31.406 align:middle
But you're right to
point to the last report,

17:31.406 --> 17:35.176 align:middle
where it was your-job losses in
the household survey-job gains,

17:35.216 --> 17:37.756 align:middle
big job gains in the
establishment survey.

17:38.376 --> 17:42.276 align:middle
So, I mean, we're left
with ambiguous results,

17:42.276 --> 17:44.406 align:middle
and we have to deal with
that uncertainty around data.

17:45.086 --> 17:47.896 align:middle
Nonetheless, the overall
picture is one of a strong

17:48.446 --> 17:51.906 align:middle
and gradually cooling-gradually
rebalancing labor market.

17:52.386 --> 17:55.936 align:middle
Job openings, while they've come
way down, are still, you know,

17:55.936 --> 17:57.916 align:middle
greater than the number
of unemployed people.

17:58.066 --> 18:01.686 align:middle
The jobs-workers gap is still
a significantly positive

18:01.736 --> 18:03.616 align:middle
number-greater than it
was before the pandemic.

18:03.616 --> 18:05.316 align:middle
So, overall, we're looking

18:05.316 --> 18:07.516 align:middle
at what is still a very
strong labor market

18:07.516 --> 18:10.186 align:middle
but not the superheated
labor market

18:10.186 --> 18:11.976 align:middle
of two years ago or
even one year ago.

18:13.136 --> 18:14.716 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:15.276 --> 18:15.626 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

18:15.966 --> 18:16.936 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

18:16.936 --> 18:18.036 align:middle
Neil Irwin, with Axios.

18:18.036 --> 18:22.186 align:middle
Back on the rates path: The
SEP showed quite a large shift

18:22.326 --> 18:23.966 align:middle
of rate cut expectations
relative

18:23.966 --> 18:25.476 align:middle
to March-that's a period

18:25.476 --> 18:27.196 align:middle
in which the economic
data flow hasn't been

18:27.236 --> 18:28.756 align:middle
that dramatic a shift.

18:28.756 --> 18:31.926 align:middle
How, how-can you provide
some color on what changed

18:31.926 --> 18:33.116 align:middle
in the attitudes
on the Committee

18:33.116 --> 18:36.576 align:middle
over the last three months to,
to see a much shallower path

18:36.576 --> 18:37.486 align:middle
of rate cuts this year?

18:37.486 --> 18:37.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:37.706 --> 18:39.876 align:middle
Yeah. The big thing that changed
was the inflation forecast.

18:40.426 --> 18:43.056 align:middle
So the inflation forecast
moved up several tenths

18:43.606 --> 18:44.516 align:middle
for the end of the year.

18:44.776 --> 18:49.596 align:middle
And, and, as I mentioned
earlier, in the, you know-what,

18:50.476 --> 18:53.076 align:middle
what did we take away from this?

18:53.076 --> 18:55.156 align:middle
We had really, really
good inflation data

18:55.156 --> 18:57.376 align:middle
in the second half of last
year, then kind of a pause

18:57.376 --> 18:58.596 align:middle
in progress in the
first quarter.

18:58.596 --> 19:01.076 align:middle
And what we took away from that
was that it's probably going

19:01.076 --> 19:03.746 align:middle
to take longer to get the
confidence that we need

19:03.746 --> 19:06.456 align:middle
to begin to loosen policy.

19:06.726 --> 19:10.766 align:middle
So the sense of that
is that rate cuts

19:10.796 --> 19:13.446 align:middle
that might take place
in-might've taken place this

19:13.446 --> 19:15.296 align:middle
year-take place next
year, you know?

19:15.296 --> 19:18.066 align:middle
We, we-there are-there
are fewer rate cuts

19:18.066 --> 19:21.136 align:middle
in the median this year, but
there's one more next year.

19:21.286 --> 19:25.806 align:middle
So, so you really-if you look at
year-end 2025 and '26, you're,

19:25.806 --> 19:27.676 align:middle
you're almost exactly
where you would have been,

19:27.676 --> 19:30.496 align:middle
just it-just it's moved later
because of that progress.

19:30.786 --> 19:33.116 align:middle
Now, you get another-you get
data-different data today.

19:33.116 --> 19:35.526 align:middle
So we'll have to see where
the data light the way.

19:35.676 --> 19:39.946 align:middle
You know, we're, we're-the
economy has, you know,

19:39.946 --> 19:43.226 align:middle
repeatedly surprised
forecasters in both directions,

19:43.226 --> 19:46.146 align:middle
and today was certainly
a better inflation report

19:46.146 --> 19:47.386 align:middle
than almost anybody expected.

19:48.046 --> 19:49.596 align:middle
And we'll just have to see what,

19:49.666 --> 19:53.266 align:middle
what-what the incoming
data flow brings and,

19:53.266 --> 19:55.376 align:middle
and how that affects the outlook
and the balance of risks.

19:55.886 --> 19:56.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:57.516 --> 20:00.506 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

20:02.106 --> 20:03.816 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek, New York Times.

20:03.816 --> 20:05.856 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

20:06.996 --> 20:08.916 align:middle
In the Summary of
Economic Projections,

20:08.966 --> 20:12.066 align:middle
the long-run interest rate
forecast moved up a bit.

20:12.066 --> 20:15.546 align:middle
I wonder if we should read
that as a sign that you think

20:15.626 --> 20:18.686 align:middle
that policy is not
sort of as restrictive

20:18.686 --> 20:20.136 align:middle
as we've previously expected.

20:20.136 --> 20:22.566 align:middle
You know, how should we
interpret what that means

20:22.566 --> 20:24.606 align:middle
about how the Committee views
its current policy setting?

20:25.136 --> 20:25.496 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:25.496 --> 20:27.926 align:middle
So two things about that-one,

20:27.926 --> 20:31.316 align:middle
one is this-you're
right, it did move up.

20:31.626 --> 20:33.416 align:middle
But, but I want to
remember to point

20:33.416 --> 20:37.026 align:middle
out that the long-run
neutral rate of interest is,

20:37.086 --> 20:38.276 align:middle
is a long-run concept.

20:38.276 --> 20:41.226 align:middle
It, it really is a
theoretical concept-can't be

20:41.226 --> 20:42.116 align:middle
directly observed.

20:42.116 --> 20:44.576 align:middle
And what it is is
the, the interest rate

20:44.576 --> 20:47.256 align:middle
that would hold the economy at
equilibrium-maximum employment

20:47.256 --> 20:50.546 align:middle
and price stability-potentially,
potentially years in the future

20:50.546 --> 20:51.746 align:middle
where there are no shocks.

20:52.106 --> 20:55.306 align:middle
So it's-it's a little bit-it's
not something we observe today.

20:55.636 --> 20:58.116 align:middle
Today we've got a very
specific economy with all kinds

20:58.116 --> 21:00.416 align:middle
of shocks we're still getting
over from the pandemic.

21:00.956 --> 21:04.696 align:middle
So it's-I, I don't
think that the,

21:04.696 --> 21:06.996 align:middle
the concept of r-star-it's
a very important concept

21:06.996 --> 21:08.496 align:middle
in economics and in what we do.

21:08.926 --> 21:11.896 align:middle
But, honestly, it doesn't really
get you where you need to be

21:11.946 --> 21:15.466 align:middle
to think about what appropriate
policy is in the near term.

21:15.996 --> 21:18.516 align:middle
But I, I-back to your
original question,

21:19.096 --> 21:20.726 align:middle
people have been
gradually writing it

21:20.726 --> 21:23.106 align:middle
up because I just think
people are coming to the view

21:23.546 --> 21:27.086 align:middle
that rates aren't-are less
likely to go down to their,

21:27.756 --> 21:29.986 align:middle
their pre-pandemic levels,
which were, you know,

21:29.986 --> 21:32.726 align:middle
very low by recent
history measures.

21:32.726 --> 21:34.536 align:middle
Now, we, we can't
really know that.

21:35.096 --> 21:37.546 align:middle
That's, that's an interesting
dispute and discussion

21:38.086 --> 21:41.096 align:middle
to have now, but, ultimately,
we think that things

21:41.096 --> 21:42.686 align:middle
like the neutral rate are driven

21:42.686 --> 21:44.866 align:middle
by longer-run, slow-moving
forces.

21:45.156 --> 21:47.846 align:middle
And, you know, there's a really
good question about whether,

21:48.306 --> 21:50.636 align:middle
whether those really have
changed or whether, instead,

21:51.026 --> 21:53.776 align:middle
rates and the economy
are experiencing a,

21:53.776 --> 21:57.616 align:middle
a series of persistent but,
ultimately, temporary shocks.

21:58.116 --> 22:01.106 align:middle
That's been the debate,
and we can't know.

22:01.106 --> 22:04.256 align:middle
But in the meantime, we're
making policy with the economy

22:04.256 --> 22:07.526 align:middle
that we have-with the
distortions that we have.

22:07.526 --> 22:09.946 align:middle
And I, I also-to
your other point, I,

22:09.946 --> 22:13.916 align:middle
I think it is-it is the case
that as time has gone by,

22:14.266 --> 22:16.146 align:middle
the question of "How
restrictive is policy?"

22:16.146 --> 22:18.096 align:middle
has become one that
everyone's asking,

22:18.096 --> 22:19.116 align:middle
and we're asking it, too.

22:19.606 --> 22:22.706 align:middle
And, you know, my answer has
been that policy is restrictive.

22:22.706 --> 22:24.806 align:middle
The question of whether it's
sufficiently restrictive is

22:24.806 --> 22:26.576 align:middle
going to be one we,
we know over time.

22:26.576 --> 22:28.456 align:middle
But I think for the
reasons I've talked

22:28.456 --> 22:29.866 align:middle
about at the last
press conference and,

22:30.396 --> 22:32.506 align:middle
and other places, I think
the evidence is pretty clear

22:32.506 --> 22:35.416 align:middle
that policy is restrictive
and is having, you know,

22:35.416 --> 22:36.956 align:middle
the effects that
we would hope for.

22:38.526 --> 22:41.316 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:41.316 --> 22:42.306 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

22:42.366 --> 22:42.876 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

22:42.876 --> 22:43.786 align:middle
Howard Schneider, with Reuters.

22:43.786 --> 22:45.466 align:middle
Just to-just to carry
that a little further,

22:45.986 --> 22:48.936 align:middle
should we read this as a
conclusion that the combination

22:48.936 --> 22:51.386 align:middle
of tipping to one cut this year

22:51.896 --> 22:55.416 align:middle
and acknowledging further
progress on inflation as kind

22:55.416 --> 22:58.106 align:middle
of a mark to market on the
level of restrictiveness

22:58.146 --> 22:59.946 align:middle
that you need-that you,
in fact, have concluded

22:59.946 --> 23:02.306 align:middle
that you weren't
quite there yet?

23:03.476 --> 23:04.376 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:04.506 --> 23:08.456 align:middle
I'd be reluctant to try
to draw that conclusion.

23:08.456 --> 23:11.826 align:middle
I, I think this is
about, you know,

23:11.826 --> 23:14.506 align:middle
looking at the incoming
data and asking,

23:14.666 --> 23:16.836 align:middle
"How much progress are
we making on inflation,

23:16.836 --> 23:18.746 align:middle
and how is the rest
of the economy doing?

23:18.746 --> 23:20.126 align:middle
Is the labor market
still strong?"

23:20.576 --> 23:23.176 align:middle
That's what we're
thinking about.

23:23.176 --> 23:26.236 align:middle
You know, you can-you
can translate it

23:26.236 --> 23:28.216 align:middle
into the-into the language
you're talking about, but,

23:28.216 --> 23:31.286 align:middle
to me, the focus is
more on, we have a goal,

23:31.286 --> 23:33.596 align:middle
which is price stability,
and another goal,

23:33.596 --> 23:34.756 align:middle
which is maximum employment.

23:35.196 --> 23:36.146 align:middle
How are we doing there?

23:36.266 --> 23:38.436 align:middle
We think we've got a good,
strong labor market still.

23:39.106 --> 23:42.546 align:middle
We think we've been
making progress toward the

23:42.546 --> 23:43.556 align:middle
price-stability goal.

23:43.826 --> 23:45.336 align:middle
And then for a while,
there was a pause.

23:45.336 --> 23:47.596 align:middle
And we look at today's
thing, and we think, "Well,

23:47.756 --> 23:50.786 align:middle
that's a good reading, and we
hope we get more like that."

23:50.786 --> 23:52.716 align:middle
And, you know, in the
meantime, we're asking,

23:52.716 --> 23:54.506 align:middle
"Is our policy stance
about right?"

23:54.506 --> 23:56.976 align:middle
And I think we think,
"Yes, it's about right."

23:57.106 --> 23:59.366 align:middle
We're prepared to adjust
that as appropriate,

23:59.366 --> 24:01.706 align:middle
but we think-we think
we're getting the things

24:01.706 --> 24:03.556 align:middle
that we would want to
get, broadly speaking.

24:03.996 --> 24:04.696 align:middle
And that's why we've been

24:04.696 --> 24:06.796 align:middle
at this policy rate
now for almost a year.

24:07.196 --> 24:07.636 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

24:07.686 --> 24:09.356 align:middle
So, so just to follow
up on that-I think one,

24:09.516 --> 24:10.696 align:middle
one curious thing here is,

24:10.746 --> 24:13.276 align:middle
you've got now this
restrictive policy in place

24:13.816 --> 24:15.446 align:middle
and virtually no change in any

24:15.446 --> 24:18.096 align:middle
of the major things
for all of this year.

24:18.146 --> 24:20.286 align:middle
You've got growth that stays

24:20.286 --> 24:23.406 align:middle
above long-run potential
throughout the forecast period,

24:23.886 --> 24:25.836 align:middle
unemployment that never goes

24:25.836 --> 24:29.506 align:middle
above the long-run
estimate-right?-of 4.2.

24:29.816 --> 24:33.026 align:middle
So this isn't a story of
slack improving inflation.

24:33.146 --> 24:35.026 align:middle
So where's the improvement
in inflation coming

24:35.076 --> 24:38.356 align:middle
from that it's going to pick
up pace so much in 2025?

24:38.356 --> 24:38.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:38.616 --> 24:41.196 align:middle
Well, you know, it's been
coming-where's it been coming

24:41.196 --> 24:42.206 align:middle
from-let's start with that.

24:42.606 --> 24:44.676 align:middle
You know, clearly
a lot of what-some

24:44.806 --> 24:46.816 align:middle
of what we've been getting
is just the reversal

24:46.816 --> 24:49.866 align:middle
of the-the unwinding of the
pandemic-related distortions

24:49.866 --> 24:51.206 align:middle
to both supply and demand.

24:51.756 --> 24:53.826 align:middle
And that is, you
know-that is complemented by

24:53.826 --> 24:57.266 align:middle
and amplified-supported by
restrictive monetary policy.

24:57.296 --> 24:58.776 align:middle
So those two things have
been working together.

24:58.936 --> 25:02.206 align:middle
We've also had a very positive
supply shock on the labor side.

25:02.206 --> 25:04.716 align:middle
And, and, also, you get-you
get a positive supply shock

25:04.716 --> 25:07.266 align:middle
when the-when the
supply conditions unwind

25:07.266 --> 25:08.426 align:middle
and return to normal.

25:08.426 --> 25:09.576 align:middle
So you've had, you know,

25:09.986 --> 25:13.976 align:middle
above-trend potential
growth and, and high growth.

25:14.596 --> 25:17.176 align:middle
And yet you've had the benefit
of, of inflation coming down,

25:17.176 --> 25:19.316 align:middle
you know, really fast,
actually, last year,

25:19.316 --> 25:22.166 align:middle
and you will see what the rest
of this year brinks-brings.

25:22.536 --> 25:25.326 align:middle
So these dynamics can continue
as long as they continue.

25:25.326 --> 25:27.696 align:middle
I mean, ultimately, the
question is, ultimately,

25:28.386 --> 25:30.326 align:middle
are you down to demand?

25:30.616 --> 25:31.986 align:middle
And, and we don't
know that, though.

25:31.986 --> 25:34.186 align:middle
I mean, look at today's report.

25:34.186 --> 25:36.826 align:middle
You know, if we see
more like that-you know,

25:36.826 --> 25:38.856 align:middle
we don't-we can't know
what the future holds.

25:38.886 --> 25:41.066 align:middle
But in the meantime, we've
made pretty good progress

25:41.066 --> 25:43.386 align:middle
on inflation with
our current stance.

25:43.556 --> 25:44.696 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:45.286 --> 25:47.556 align:middle
Mike. MICHAEL MCKEE.

25:48.396 --> 25:50.796 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

25:51.276 --> 25:54.636 align:middle
The base case of the Committee
seems to be that there is going

25:54.636 --> 25:57.246 align:middle
to be at least one
rate cut this year,

25:57.656 --> 26:02.666 align:middle
but your growth forecast doesn't
see any slowdown in the rest

26:02.666 --> 26:05.096 align:middle
of the year, nor does the
unemployment forecast see any

26:05.096 --> 26:08.586 align:middle
significant weakening
of the labor market.

26:08.976 --> 26:12.016 align:middle
And your inflation
forecasts basically average

26:12.016 --> 26:13.336 align:middle
out to no change.

26:13.696 --> 26:15.926 align:middle
So if, at the end of the
year, there is no change

26:15.926 --> 26:21.656 align:middle
from conditions now, why would
you anticipate cutting rates?

26:21.656 --> 26:24.416 align:middle
What, what would be the
point for a rate cut?

26:24.416 --> 26:24.626 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:24.626 --> 26:26.536 align:middle
Well, we think-we think
policy is restrictive.

26:27.046 --> 26:31.296 align:middle
And we think, ultimately, that
if you-if you just set policy

26:31.296 --> 26:32.216 align:middle
at a restrictive level,

26:32.216 --> 26:34.586 align:middle
eventually you will see real
weakening in the economy.

26:34.586 --> 26:37.766 align:middle
So that's always been the
thought is that, you know,

26:37.766 --> 26:39.516 align:middle
since we raise rates
this far, we've,

26:39.776 --> 26:42.216 align:middle
we've always been pointing
to cuts at a certain point.

26:43.026 --> 26:45.426 align:middle
Not to eliminate the possibility
of hikes, but, you know,

26:45.426 --> 26:46.986 align:middle
no one has that as
their base case,

26:47.366 --> 26:50.256 align:middle
so-no one on the Committee does.

26:50.326 --> 26:53.186 align:middle
But so that's, that's-you know,
that's how we think about it.

26:53.186 --> 26:54.456 align:middle
And that's what we've
been getting.

26:54.456 --> 26:57.296 align:middle
That's what we've been getting
is good progress on inflation,

26:57.706 --> 27:02.166 align:middle
with growth at a-at
a, a good level

27:02.246 --> 27:03.406 align:middle
and with a strong labor market.

27:03.406 --> 27:06.766 align:middle
Now, ultimately, we think
rates will have to come

27:06.766 --> 27:08.116 align:middle
down to continue
to support that.

27:08.686 --> 27:10.946 align:middle
But, so far, they
haven't had to.

27:11.206 --> 27:13.236 align:middle
And, you know, that's why
we're watching so carefully

27:13.236 --> 27:14.236 align:middle
for signs of weakness.

27:14.856 --> 27:15.676 align:middle
We don't really see that.

27:15.676 --> 27:19.146 align:middle
We kind of see what we wanted to
see, which was gradual cooling

27:19.146 --> 27:20.926 align:middle
in demand-gradual rebalancing

27:20.926 --> 27:22.286 align:middle
in the labor market
while we're continuing

27:22.286 --> 27:23.556 align:middle
to make progress on inflation.

27:24.116 --> 27:26.956 align:middle
So we're getting-we're
getting good results here.

27:26.956 --> 27:27.626 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

27:27.626 --> 27:30.556 align:middle
But, to follow up: Is
there any kind of concern

27:30.556 --> 27:31.936 align:middle
for the housing industry

27:31.936 --> 27:35.966 align:middle
or financial stability-banks-in
leaving rates where they are

27:36.056 --> 27:37.356 align:middle
for too long at this point?

27:39.186 --> 27:39.956 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:40.136 --> 27:44.086 align:middle
On housing-you know, the housing
situation is a complicated one.

27:44.286 --> 27:46.766 align:middle
And you can see that's a place
where rates are really having,

27:46.766 --> 27:47.936 align:middle
having a significant effect.

27:48.596 --> 27:51.486 align:middle
I mean, ultimately, the
best thing we can do

27:51.486 --> 27:54.936 align:middle
for the housing market is
to bring inflation down so

27:54.936 --> 27:56.596 align:middle
that we can bring rates down so

27:56.596 --> 27:58.766 align:middle
that the housing market
can continue to normalize.

27:58.766 --> 28:01.626 align:middle
There will still be a
national housing shortage,

28:01.626 --> 28:03.396 align:middle
as there was before
the pandemic.

28:03.396 --> 28:06.246 align:middle
There will still be one, but
the distortions that we see now

28:06.246 --> 28:08.266 align:middle
with lock-ins and things
like that-you know,

28:08.316 --> 28:11.916 align:middle
low mortgages-in terms of banks,
the banking system has been,

28:11.916 --> 28:15.276 align:middle
you know, solid,
strong, well capitalized.

28:15.526 --> 28:19.066 align:middle
Lending-you know, we've seen
good performance by the banks.

28:19.306 --> 28:22.416 align:middle
We had the turmoil earlier
last year, but, you know,

28:22.416 --> 28:25.266 align:middle
banks have been focusing on
bringing up their liquidity,

28:25.266 --> 28:26.296 align:middle
bringing up their capital,

28:26.296 --> 28:28.076 align:middle
and having a
risk-risk-management plans

28:28.076 --> 28:28.586 align:middle
in place.

28:29.076 --> 28:30.566 align:middle
So the banking system, you know,

28:30.756 --> 28:32.586 align:middle
seems to be-seems
to be in good shape.

28:34.906 --> 28:35.986 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:37.616 --> 28:39.326 align:middle
Chris. CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

28:39.326 --> 28:41.236 align:middle
Thanks. Chris Rugaber
at Associated Press.

28:41.236 --> 28:45.876 align:middle
I was wondering if we
can read-on inflation,

28:45.876 --> 28:46.836 align:middle
if you can tell us a little more

28:46.836 --> 28:49.626 align:middle
about where you see inflationary
pressure in the economy.

28:50.146 --> 28:53.016 align:middle
You mentioned labor markets
coming into better balance,

28:53.436 --> 28:56.226 align:middle
and inflation expectations
appear to be well anchored.

28:56.656 --> 28:58.566 align:middle
You're seeing, you know,
anecdotal stories of,

28:58.566 --> 28:59.866 align:middle
the large chains like Walmart

28:59.866 --> 29:03.536 align:middle
and Target are announcing price
cuts-McDonald's announcing a $5

29:03.846 --> 29:04.696 align:middle
meal deal.

29:05.416 --> 29:07.666 align:middle
So people may still be
unhappy about prices

29:07.666 --> 29:10.316 align:middle
at the grocery store, but it
doesn't seem like there's a lot

29:10.316 --> 29:12.616 align:middle
of inflationary pressure left
in this economy-and wonder

29:12.686 --> 29:14.266 align:middle
if you could tell
us more about that.

29:14.266 --> 29:14.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:14.516 --> 29:15.396 align:middle
So I, I think it's true

29:15.396 --> 29:17.976 align:middle
that housing-that inflationary
pressures have come down.

29:18.166 --> 29:20.556 align:middle
But we still have-we're still
getting high inflation readings.

29:20.556 --> 29:22.656 align:middle
So, you know-and I
think you can see it in,

29:22.656 --> 29:26.726 align:middle
in various places in-you
know, in some parts of,

29:26.796 --> 29:28.696 align:middle
of nonhousing services.

29:28.696 --> 29:32.576 align:middle
You see elevated inflation
still-and that's probably

29:32.576 --> 29:35.166 align:middle
to do with-it could
be to do with wages.

29:35.666 --> 29:38.616 align:middle
Goods prices have
kind of fluctuated.

29:38.866 --> 29:42.826 align:middle
There's been a surprising
increase in, in import prices

29:42.826 --> 29:45.196 align:middle
on goods, which is kind
of hard to understand.

29:45.746 --> 29:49.026 align:middle
And, you know, may, may-we've
taken some signal from that,

29:49.086 --> 29:52.426 align:middle
but, you know-and, and, of
course, housing services.

29:52.426 --> 29:53.906 align:middle
You're seeing-you're continuing

29:53.906 --> 29:56.676 align:middle
to see high readings
there to some extent.

29:56.676 --> 29:58.856 align:middle
That's, that's, you
know, catch-up inflation

29:58.856 --> 29:59.886 align:middle
from earlier pressures.

29:59.916 --> 30:01.596 align:middle
But, I mean, overall,
you're right.

30:02.126 --> 30:03.766 align:middle
Inflationary pressures
have come down.

30:04.406 --> 30:07.616 align:middle
As I mentioned, the labor market
has come into better balance.

30:07.916 --> 30:12.616 align:middle
Wages are still running, you
know, I would say, above a,

30:12.716 --> 30:15.496 align:middle
a sustainable path, which would
be that of trend inflation

30:15.496 --> 30:16.496 align:middle
and trend productivity.

30:17.046 --> 30:19.456 align:middle
You're still seeing wage
increases moving above that.

30:19.456 --> 30:21.716 align:middle
We, we haven't thought of
wages as being the principal,

30:22.366 --> 30:24.206 align:middle
you know, cause of inflation.

30:24.206 --> 30:25.486 align:middle
But at the same time,
getting back

30:25.486 --> 30:29.116 align:middle
to 2 percent inflation is
likely to require a return

30:29.116 --> 30:30.186 align:middle
to a more sustainable level,

30:30.186 --> 30:32.286 align:middle
which is somewhat
below the current level

30:32.286 --> 30:33.506 align:middle
of increases in the aggregate.

30:34.196 --> 30:35.106 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:35.236 --> 30:36.316 align:middle
Rachel. RACHEL SIEGEL.

30:38.696 --> 30:39.506 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

30:39.506 --> 30:40.816 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

30:40.886 --> 30:41.936 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

30:42.716 --> 30:43.926 align:middle
There's obviously a lot of focus

30:43.966 --> 30:46.586 align:middle
on how many cuts could
be expected this year.

30:46.776 --> 30:49.706 align:middle
But can you give us a sense
of what one cut by the end

30:49.706 --> 30:50.836 align:middle
of the year would actually do

30:50.836 --> 30:53.466 align:middle
to the economy-what you think
would be a meaningful difference

30:53.466 --> 30:55.376 align:middle
if there was a cut by the
end of the year or even two?

30:55.856 --> 30:56.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:56.336 --> 30:59.326 align:middle
I think if, you know-if you
look back in 5 or 10 years and,

30:59.326 --> 31:02.126 align:middle
and try to pull out the
significance to the U.S. economy

31:02.126 --> 31:04.066 align:middle
of one 25 basis point
rate cut, you'd,

31:04.156 --> 31:06.746 align:middle
you'd have quite a
job on your hands.

31:06.746 --> 31:09.536 align:middle
So that's not how we look
at it-you know, really,

31:09.536 --> 31:11.646 align:middle
the whole rate path matters.

31:11.646 --> 31:15.256 align:middle
And I, I do continue to
think that when-you know,

31:15.256 --> 31:20.036 align:middle
when we do start to loosen
policy, that will show

31:20.036 --> 31:22.846 align:middle
up in a significant loosening
in financial market conditions.

31:23.286 --> 31:25.526 align:middle
And the market will price
in what it prices in.

31:25.526 --> 31:27.466 align:middle
I don't-I have no
way of saying-we're,

31:27.466 --> 31:28.596 align:middle
we're not at that stage,

31:28.596 --> 31:32.166 align:middle
so I don't know what we'll be
thinking about at that time.

31:32.166 --> 31:35.876 align:middle
But it's a consequential
decision for the economy.

31:35.876 --> 31:38.646 align:middle
And, you know, you want
to-you want to get it right.

31:38.646 --> 31:40.996 align:middle
And, fortunately, we have
a strong economy, and we,

31:41.036 --> 31:43.516 align:middle
we have the ability
to, you know,

31:43.586 --> 31:44.986 align:middle
approach this question
carefully.

31:44.986 --> 31:47.816 align:middle
And we will approach it
carefully while we're very much

31:47.816 --> 31:50.816 align:middle
keeping an eye on, you know,
downside economic risks,

31:50.816 --> 31:51.946 align:middle
should they-should they emerge.

31:52.756 --> 31:53.056 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

31:53.056 --> 31:55.276 align:middle
And you've been talking
about this clear sense

31:55.276 --> 31:56.546 align:middle
that it's just going
to take longer

31:56.716 --> 31:58.926 align:middle
to have the confidence
that's needed for rate cuts.

31:59.006 --> 32:00.996 align:middle
Is there something about what
happened in the first couple

32:00.996 --> 32:03.246 align:middle
of months of the year that
you're seeing differently now

32:03.246 --> 32:04.426 align:middle
with more time or hindsight?

32:04.426 --> 32:07.606 align:middle
Do you still characterize
it as expected bumpiness

32:07.606 --> 32:10.046 align:middle
or something more lasting
that is affecting your policy

32:10.046 --> 32:11.336 align:middle
for the rest of the year?

32:11.336 --> 32:11.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:11.876 --> 32:14.866 align:middle
You know, we always want
to avoid the tendency

32:14.866 --> 32:18.036 align:middle
to dismiss the parts of
inflation that we-that we don't

32:18.036 --> 32:19.926 align:middle
like and just make it go away.

32:19.926 --> 32:22.736 align:middle
So-but we had a quarter where
inflation was running higher,

32:22.736 --> 32:24.436 align:middle
and, yes, I could
stand here and say,

32:24.846 --> 32:27.476 align:middle
"It's stuff we shouldn't
have taken signal from."

32:27.476 --> 32:28.336 align:middle
But it is what it is.

32:28.336 --> 32:30.246 align:middle
You know, you, you
have to-you know,

32:30.246 --> 32:31.576 align:middle
low inflation is low inflation.

32:31.576 --> 32:34.976 align:middle
High-so if you have a quarter
where it's higher-we tried not

32:34.976 --> 32:36.726 align:middle
to take signal from the
first couple of months,

32:36.756 --> 32:38.406 align:middle
but we got a third
month, and we said, "Okay,

32:38.826 --> 32:42.486 align:middle
the signal we're taking is that,
you know, we think it's going

32:42.486 --> 32:44.106 align:middle
to take longer to get confidence

32:44.146 --> 32:46.056 align:middle
that inflation is moving
sustainably down to 2 percent."

32:46.206 --> 32:47.236 align:middle
That's, that's what we said.

32:47.866 --> 32:49.076 align:middle
I think that was the
right thing to do.

32:49.116 --> 32:50.326 align:middle
I still think that's
the right thing to do.

32:50.616 --> 32:52.396 align:middle
Now we have today's
inflation reading,

32:52.396 --> 32:54.436 align:middle
which is very different-very
much more positive.

32:54.926 --> 32:56.656 align:middle
We're going to have to see
what the trend is-what's,

32:56.656 --> 32:58.236 align:middle
what's going to be the
data going forward.

32:58.686 --> 33:00.766 align:middle
We're looking for something
that gives us confidence

33:00.766 --> 33:03.756 align:middle
that inflation is, is moving
sustainably down to 2 percent.

33:04.086 --> 33:06.446 align:middle
And readings like today's,
you know-that's a step

33:06.446 --> 33:07.186 align:middle
in the right direction.

33:07.186 --> 33:10.786 align:middle
But, you know, one reading
is-isn't-it just-it's only

33:10.786 --> 33:11.286 align:middle
one reading.

33:11.286 --> 33:13.126 align:middle
You don't want to-you don't
want to be too motivated

33:13.126 --> 33:14.256 align:middle
by any single data point.

33:15.016 --> 33:15.366 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:15.366 --> 33:18.806 align:middle
Jo Ling. JO LING KENT.

33:19.496 --> 33:20.376 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

33:20.376 --> 33:21.716 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

33:21.716 --> 33:23.576 align:middle
I'm Jo Ling Kent, with CBS News.

33:25.056 --> 33:26.826 align:middle
What's your message to Americans

33:26.826 --> 33:28.996 align:middle
who are seeing encouraging
economic data

33:29.186 --> 33:31.416 align:middle
but don't feel good
about this economy?

33:33.396 --> 33:33.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:33.816 --> 33:38.506 align:middle
I, you know-I, I don't think
anyone knows-has a definitive

33:38.506 --> 33:40.726 align:middle
answer why people
are, are not as happy

33:40.726 --> 33:44.036 align:middle
about the economy
as they might be.

33:44.036 --> 33:45.536 align:middle
And we don't tell people
how they should think

33:45.536 --> 33:46.616 align:middle
or feel about the economy.

33:46.616 --> 33:47.426 align:middle
That's not our job.

33:47.876 --> 33:50.086 align:middle
We-you know, people
experience what they experience.

33:50.566 --> 33:52.306 align:middle
All I can tell you is what
the-what the data show,

33:52.436 --> 33:55.716 align:middle
which is, we've got an economy
that's growing at a solid pace.

33:56.286 --> 33:57.866 align:middle
We've got a very
strong labor market

33:58.036 --> 34:00.036 align:middle
with unemployment at 4 percent.

34:00.036 --> 34:02.756 align:middle
It's been a long time
since we've had, you know,

34:03.046 --> 34:06.516 align:middle
a long stretch of time
with unemployment at

34:06.516 --> 34:09.526 align:middle
or below 4 percent-very
long time.

34:09.526 --> 34:11.126 align:middle
We had a period of
high inflation.

34:11.656 --> 34:14.606 align:middle
We-inflation has come
down really significantly,

34:14.606 --> 34:18.666 align:middle
and we're doing everything
we can to-you know,

34:18.666 --> 34:21.476 align:middle
to bring that inflationary
episode fully to a halt

34:21.476 --> 34:23.036 align:middle
and fully restore
price stability.

34:23.336 --> 34:24.596 align:middle
We're confident that
we'll get there.

34:25.176 --> 34:27.896 align:middle
And in the meantime, you know,
it's going to be painful for,

34:27.896 --> 34:30.146 align:middle
for people, but the
ultimate pain would be a,

34:30.146 --> 34:33.126 align:middle
a period of long, high-a long
period of high inflation.

34:33.126 --> 34:36.106 align:middle
It is people who-lower-income
people-people who are

34:36.106 --> 34:37.656 align:middle
at the margins of
the economy who,

34:38.096 --> 34:40.836 align:middle
who have the worst
experience-who experience the

34:40.836 --> 34:42.096 align:middle
most pain from inflation.

34:42.226 --> 34:44.996 align:middle
So, you know, it's for those
people-for all Americans,

34:44.996 --> 34:46.506 align:middle
but particularly for those
people-that we're doing

34:46.506 --> 34:48.106 align:middle
everything we can to
bring inflation back

34:48.106 --> 34:48.826 align:middle
down under control.

34:49.206 --> 34:49.546 align:middle
JO LING KENT.

34:49.546 --> 34:52.426 align:middle
As just a quick follow-up
here-you've indicated one

34:52.566 --> 34:54.386 align:middle
interest rate cut
sometime this year.

34:54.386 --> 34:56.396 align:middle
And I know you don't have
a crystal ball up there,

34:56.396 --> 34:59.776 align:middle
but a lot of people are
watching and see, you know,

34:59.826 --> 35:02.256 align:middle
borrowing money remains
very expensive.

35:02.596 --> 35:05.806 align:middle
For everybody who's out
there waiting on a rate cut,

35:05.806 --> 35:09.056 align:middle
about when can consumers
expect some relief?

35:09.816 --> 35:10.086 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:10.086 --> 35:12.946 align:middle
Well, you know, I, I don't
have a precise date for you.

35:13.016 --> 35:15.176 align:middle
But what we said is that
we, we want to make sure

35:15.176 --> 35:16.586 align:middle
that we're confident

35:16.586 --> 35:19.116 align:middle
that inflation is actually
moving back down to 2 percent.

35:20.006 --> 35:22.846 align:middle
And when we are, then we can
look at loosening policy.

35:23.316 --> 35:26.946 align:middle
So having that kind
of confidence

35:26.946 --> 35:27.776 align:middle
that inflation will be

35:27.776 --> 35:31.576 align:middle
at 2 percent just-it just pays
benefits to the whole economy,

35:31.576 --> 35:34.166 align:middle
to all Americans, for
a long period of time.

35:34.546 --> 35:36.616 align:middle
We had that period
for a very long time.

35:37.036 --> 35:40.016 align:middle
We very much want to get back
to a place where people can,

35:40.076 --> 35:40.796 align:middle
can not think

35:40.796 --> 35:43.186 align:middle
about inflation-it's
just not a concern

35:43.216 --> 35:45.696 align:middle
in the everyday economic
decisions that they make.

35:45.786 --> 35:48.396 align:middle
We were there for a long
time, and our goal is

35:48.396 --> 35:49.486 align:middle
to get back to that place.

35:49.486 --> 35:52.916 align:middle
And we've made good
progress, and we're just-we're

35:52.916 --> 35:54.616 align:middle
in the phase now
of just, you know,

35:54.826 --> 35:56.306 align:middle
sticking with it
until we get it done.

35:57.926 --> 35:58.836 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:59.006 --> 36:00.706 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

36:01.286 --> 36:02.896 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

36:02.896 --> 36:05.506 align:middle
I'm just drawing on the change
in the statement on there being

36:05.926 --> 36:08.356 align:middle
"modest further progress"
on inflation.

36:09.466 --> 36:11.496 align:middle
You know, halfway, you
know, through the year

36:11.496 --> 36:14.446 align:middle
and after today's CPI release,
you know, what have you

36:14.446 --> 36:16.196 align:middle
and other Committee
members found

36:16.196 --> 36:19.386 align:middle
that has been particularly
encouraging to you?

36:19.996 --> 36:22.666 align:middle
And just in terms
of the conservatism,

36:23.126 --> 36:26.116 align:middle
how much of that
is about stickiness

36:26.116 --> 36:28.486 align:middle
and shelter inflation
in particular?

36:28.936 --> 36:29.246 align:middle
Thank you.

36:29.466 --> 36:29.786 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:29.876 --> 36:36.406 align:middle
So, encouraging has been that
growth-you know, the growth,

36:36.456 --> 36:38.986 align:middle
clearly, that we had last year,
particularly the second half

36:38.986 --> 36:40.416 align:middle
of last year, and we continue

36:40.416 --> 36:43.456 align:middle
to see still-strong
growth-solid growth this year.

36:43.456 --> 36:44.686 align:middle
That's been very encouraging.

36:45.136 --> 36:49.076 align:middle
If you go back a year, there was
a real concern about, you know,

36:49.076 --> 36:50.826 align:middle
very much slowing growth and,

36:50.826 --> 36:53.116 align:middle
and a recession-many
forecasters had that.

36:53.776 --> 36:54.976 align:middle
And that's not what happened.

36:54.976 --> 36:57.726 align:middle
Instead, we-the U.S. is,
anyway, and it's in-it's

36:57.726 --> 37:00.236 align:middle
in sharp distinction with
many other advanced economies

37:00.236 --> 37:00.756 align:middle
around the world.

37:00.756 --> 37:02.856 align:middle
So that's been encouraging.

37:02.856 --> 37:03.666 align:middle
I would say that the,

37:03.666 --> 37:06.626 align:middle
the-today's inflation report is
encouraging, but it comes after,

37:07.226 --> 37:10.416 align:middle
you know, several reports
that were not so encouraging.

37:11.056 --> 37:15.376 align:middle
You asked about-your
second question was really

37:15.376 --> 37:16.506 align:middle
about shelter inflation.

37:16.736 --> 37:22.126 align:middle
So I think if you go back
a couple of years-we,

37:22.126 --> 37:26.036 align:middle
we know that there were renters,
and then there are, you know,

37:26.156 --> 37:27.486 align:middle
people who own their houses.

37:27.486 --> 37:30.466 align:middle
And we have OER, which is
owners' equivalent rent.

37:31.046 --> 37:35.286 align:middle
And so when market-based
rents go up sharply,

37:35.286 --> 37:36.626 align:middle
as they did at the beginning

37:36.626 --> 37:39.246 align:middle
of the-of the-when
the economy reopened,

37:39.246 --> 37:40.766 align:middle
they really went up sharply.

37:41.586 --> 37:46.266 align:middle
Those play into rollover
rents much more slowly

37:46.266 --> 37:48.576 align:middle
for existing tenants than
they do for new tenants.

37:49.126 --> 37:51.976 align:middle
And, and so what we-so
we, we've found now

37:52.046 --> 37:53.526 align:middle
that there are big lags.

37:53.836 --> 37:56.636 align:middle
So there's sort of
a-there's a bulge

37:57.136 --> 38:01.246 align:middle
of high past increases
in, in market rents.

38:01.246 --> 38:03.706 align:middle
It has to get worked
off, and that may take,

38:03.846 --> 38:04.986 align:middle
you know, several years.

38:05.426 --> 38:09.176 align:middle
Nonetheless, as long as market
rents remain-are going at-up

38:09.176 --> 38:12.786 align:middle
at relatively low levels-and
they still are-you know, this,

38:12.786 --> 38:13.786 align:middle
this is just going to happen.

38:13.786 --> 38:16.526 align:middle
It just is going to happen
more slowly than we thought.

38:16.696 --> 38:19.836 align:middle
You know, we, we also
do sort of this thing

38:19.836 --> 38:25.036 align:middle
where we impute a rental
value to, to owned homes.

38:25.476 --> 38:26.906 align:middle
And that's-you know,
many countries

38:26.906 --> 38:28.756 align:middle
around the world do that.

38:29.296 --> 38:30.636 align:middle
Some do it differently than us.

38:30.636 --> 38:32.116 align:middle
It's something that
the-you know, that the,

38:32.356 --> 38:34.886 align:middle
the price experts have
regularly looked at.

38:35.456 --> 38:38.236 align:middle
It is not something that we're
the only country that does,

38:38.576 --> 38:40.336 align:middle
and it's not something
we're looking at changing

38:40.336 --> 38:43.376 align:middle
or would look at changing,
you know, anytime soon.

38:43.876 --> 38:45.316 align:middle
But it-it's true.

38:45.316 --> 38:48.416 align:middle
It's one of the very hardest
things in, in inflation

38:48.676 --> 38:52.946 align:middle
and in prices is how to think
about, you know, the services

38:52.946 --> 38:54.536 align:middle
that someone is getting
by living in a home

38:54.536 --> 38:56.816 align:middle
that they could rent, but
they're actually living in it.

38:56.816 --> 38:58.516 align:middle
And some countries-you
should just ignore that.

38:58.556 --> 39:01.816 align:middle
But that's not our-that's
not how we do inflation here.

39:02.236 --> 39:03.606 align:middle
We, we do it the way we do it,

39:03.606 --> 39:05.296 align:middle
and we've been very
transparent about it.

39:05.296 --> 39:09.136 align:middle
And, you know, it's true that
we're-we're seeing delays in,

39:09.196 --> 39:11.806 align:middle
in realizing what-what's
happening economically.

39:11.806 --> 39:14.306 align:middle
But we understand that-you know,
we understand that very well.

39:15.706 --> 39:17.296 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:17.296 --> 39:18.466 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

39:18.466 --> 39:19.396 align:middle
Edward Lawrence,
with Fox Business.

39:19.396 --> 39:20.996 align:middle
Thank you for taking the
question, Chair Powell.

39:20.996 --> 39:23.066 align:middle
So I want to go back to
jobs and the consumer.

39:23.066 --> 39:25.776 align:middle
The last jobs report showed
that over the past year,

39:25.776 --> 39:29.506 align:middle
634,000 more people
took multiple jobs.

39:30.106 --> 39:31.616 align:middle
That's up 8.2 percent.

39:31.616 --> 39:35.276 align:middle
And since January of 2021, we've
seen prices rise 19 percent.

39:35.276 --> 39:36.686 align:middle
So people are using
their credit cards

39:36.686 --> 39:37.666 align:middle
to pay for their lifestyle.

39:38.096 --> 39:40.576 align:middle
What pressure points
will signal to you

39:40.886 --> 39:43.796 align:middle
that the slowing economy could
maybe break companies in terms

39:43.796 --> 39:46.556 align:middle
of hiring or break the
consumer in terms of spending?

39:46.776 --> 39:47.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:48.776 --> 39:52.356 align:middle
So, you know, we monitor all the
things you mentioned and more.

39:52.586 --> 39:58.116 align:middle
You know, what we're seeing is,
basically, spending was going

39:58.116 --> 40:01.576 align:middle
up faster than disposable income
during big parts of next year,

40:01.576 --> 40:05.436 align:middle
so-last year, rather-and
so what that-you'd expect

40:05.436 --> 40:07.516 align:middle
to see is people spending
more on their credit cards.

40:07.586 --> 40:08.926 align:middle
That-that has been happening.

40:09.466 --> 40:10.886 align:middle
Credit card balances
have been going up.

40:10.956 --> 40:12.176 align:middle
Credit card defaults
have been going up.

40:12.256 --> 40:13.816 align:middle
They're not at high levels.

40:13.816 --> 40:17.256 align:middle
Remember that after the
pandemic, people were cooped up.

40:17.256 --> 40:19.356 align:middle
They couldn't spend money.

40:19.466 --> 40:20.616 align:middle
They couldn't go
out to spend money;

40:20.616 --> 40:21.706 align:middle
they could spend
money from home.

40:21.706 --> 40:24.006 align:middle
But households were in very,

40:24.006 --> 40:26.646 align:middle
very strong-historically
strong-financial shape.

40:26.646 --> 40:29.246 align:middle
They've now worked
off a lot of that.

40:29.316 --> 40:30.876 align:middle
So we're, we're watching
that carefully.

40:31.476 --> 40:32.566 align:middle
And, you know, what do we see?

40:32.566 --> 40:33.196 align:middle
That's what we see.

40:33.196 --> 40:35.416 align:middle
We, we see the same data
that everybody else sees,

40:35.416 --> 40:38.966 align:middle
but you've still got a household
sector that's in-that's

40:38.966 --> 40:39.826 align:middle
in pretty good shape.

40:40.276 --> 40:42.426 align:middle
You know-but, nonetheless,
it's not in the kind

40:42.426 --> 40:43.916 align:middle
of shape it was in
a year or two ago.

40:43.916 --> 40:45.586 align:middle
And we're, you know-we're
carefully watching that.

40:45.636 --> 40:46.406 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

40:46.406 --> 40:48.496 align:middle
So how close then
are we to that point

40:48.496 --> 40:50.476 align:middle
where the consumer
can't continue to spend

40:50.476 --> 40:52.426 align:middle
as it's spending or companies
can't hire like they're hiring?

40:52.456 --> 40:52.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:52.926 --> 40:54.676 align:middle
So consumer spending
is still growing.

40:54.676 --> 40:58.026 align:middle
It's not growing at the pace it
was growing at a year or so ago,

40:58.026 --> 41:00.696 align:middle
but, but it's still-it's
still growing solidly.

41:00.886 --> 41:03.146 align:middle
And, by the way, other parts
of the economy are picking up.

41:03.596 --> 41:07.296 align:middle
Spending on equipment and
intangibles is-has picked

41:07.296 --> 41:10.526 align:middle
up quite a bit, you know, in
the wake of all the construction

41:10.526 --> 41:13.826 align:middle
that we saw of new
tech-you know, tech plants.

41:14.316 --> 41:18.346 align:middle
So, overall, the economy is
exhibiting solid growth, and,

41:18.346 --> 41:20.866 align:middle
you know-something around 2
percent-which is-which is,

41:21.046 --> 41:23.356 align:middle
you know, good-a good pace of
growth for the U.S. economy.

41:24.056 --> 41:25.366 align:middle
But, no, you're,
you're right, though.

41:25.366 --> 41:28.126 align:middle
We, we do see the same thing
other people see, which is,

41:28.646 --> 41:32.526 align:middle
you know, increasing financial
pressures on, you know,

41:32.526 --> 41:35.016 align:middle
more lower-income people.

41:35.016 --> 41:38.636 align:middle
And, and, you know, the
best thing we can do is,

41:39.376 --> 41:42.136 align:middle
is to foster a very
strong jobs economy,

41:42.136 --> 41:45.106 align:middle
which we think we have
done-ultimately to get inflation

41:45.106 --> 41:47.736 align:middle
under control, because those
people experience inflation,

41:47.736 --> 41:49.446 align:middle
you know, very directly-very
painfully.

41:50.126 --> 41:52.676 align:middle
And, and, you know, once, once
we get inflation under control,

41:52.676 --> 41:55.846 align:middle
rates can come down-which,
which will also improve things.

41:55.846 --> 41:57.516 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:57.516 --> 41:59.106 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

42:00.016 --> 42:01.866 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

42:02.446 --> 42:04.766 align:middle
You've mentioned that
unexpected weakening

42:04.766 --> 42:07.896 align:middle
in the labor market would,
would bring rate cuts sooner.

42:07.896 --> 42:09.596 align:middle
And I was just wondering-can
you talk a little bit

42:09.596 --> 42:11.136 align:middle
about what that would look like?

42:11.136 --> 42:16.536 align:middle
Is it more of, like, a quicker
pace of people losing jobs?

42:16.576 --> 42:19.006 align:middle
Is it more the level of the
unemployment rate if it were

42:19.006 --> 42:22.036 align:middle
to start to go above 4.2
or something like that?

42:22.326 --> 42:23.786 align:middle
How are you thinking
about what you're looking

42:23.826 --> 42:25.856 align:middle
for to see the job market
unexpectedly weaken?

42:25.856 --> 42:26.326 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:26.586 --> 42:30.526 align:middle
You know-you know, when
I say "unexpectedly,"

42:30.526 --> 42:34.076 align:middle
the first thing is more than,
kind of, is in our forecast

42:34.076 --> 42:38.006 align:middle
or in common forecasts-so,
something more than that.

42:38.006 --> 42:39.916 align:middle
But we'll be looking
at everything.

42:39.966 --> 42:43.106 align:middle
You know, we'll-the, the
labor market, you know,

42:43.676 --> 42:46.856 align:middle
has the ability-has, has
the tendency sometimes to,

42:46.856 --> 42:48.396 align:middle
to weaken quickly.

42:49.086 --> 42:52.456 align:middle
So waiting for that to happen
is, is not what we're doing.

42:52.896 --> 42:54.806 align:middle
You know, we're watching
very carefully; we're looking

42:54.806 --> 42:55.816 align:middle
at the balance of risks.

42:55.816 --> 42:58.706 align:middle
I always point out the
balance of risks, so-and,

42:58.776 --> 43:00.306 align:middle
and also the fact
that we look at,

43:00.306 --> 43:02.256 align:middle
at all of it-the
whole situation.

43:02.256 --> 43:07.896 align:middle
So, you know, a decision
to begin to-you know,

43:08.066 --> 43:12.436 align:middle
to loosen policy could have
several reasons associated

43:12.436 --> 43:13.476 align:middle
with it at a given time.

43:13.966 --> 43:15.116 align:middle
But I, you know, I
would just-you know,

43:15.116 --> 43:17.796 align:middle
we monitor all the-all
the labor market data.

43:17.796 --> 43:21.176 align:middle
And if we saw
troubling-weakening more

43:21.176 --> 43:22.326 align:middle
than-more than expected,

43:23.006 --> 43:25.466 align:middle
then that would be something
we'd consider responding to.

43:25.466 --> 43:27.636 align:middle
But we'd look at the-we
look at the broader context

43:27.636 --> 43:28.376 align:middle
of what's going on, too.

43:28.376 --> 43:28.736 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

43:28.936 --> 43:31.126 align:middle
So something like negative
payroll numbers would be-

43:31.126 --> 43:31.386 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:31.386 --> 43:33.556 align:middle
I'm not going to-yeah, I
mean, I, I can think of things

43:33.556 --> 43:35.446 align:middle
that would-many things
would make it on that list,

43:35.446 --> 43:38.216 align:middle
but I don't think I'll,
I'll utter them here.

43:38.736 --> 43:39.806 align:middle
SIMON RABINOVITCH.

43:41.116 --> 43:44.936 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

43:45.136 --> 43:47.026 align:middle
Simon Rabinovitch
with the Economist.

43:47.186 --> 43:48.296 align:middle
May I ask-you mentioned,

43:48.296 --> 43:50.826 align:middle
with the slightly higher PCE
[inflation] readings earlier

43:50.826 --> 43:54.606 align:middle
this year, that it wasn't one
or two months but three months

43:54.956 --> 43:57.896 align:middle
that tipped you to be a bit more
cautious in the rate outlook.

43:58.636 --> 44:00.286 align:middle
Should we take from
that that-you know,

44:00.286 --> 44:02.176 align:middle
it looks like May will be
quite good for PCE [inflation],

44:02.176 --> 44:04.816 align:middle
as it was for CPI-does
that imply that kind

44:04.816 --> 44:08.386 align:middle
of two more good months would
reinstill the confidence

44:08.446 --> 44:10.156 align:middle
that rate cuts could
be coming sooner

44:10.156 --> 44:11.226 align:middle
than you currently project?

44:11.226 --> 44:11.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:11.656 --> 44:13.346 align:middle
You know, I, I don't
actually have that kind

44:13.346 --> 44:16.166 align:middle
of mechanical thing
in, in my thinking.

44:16.246 --> 44:19.086 align:middle
I, I get that, you
know-as many good ones

44:19.086 --> 44:21.076 align:middle
as we had bad ones-but
it's not like that.

44:21.076 --> 44:21.686 align:middle
It's the whole.

44:21.686 --> 44:22.936 align:middle
It's going to be the totality

44:22.936 --> 44:25.256 align:middle
of the data-[including] labor
market data, the growth data.

44:25.256 --> 44:28.636 align:middle
In terms of inflation,
you know, you'd,

44:28.636 --> 44:31.756 align:middle
you'd want to see real progress
that builds your confidence

44:31.796 --> 44:34.516 align:middle
that we're-that we are on
a path down to 2 percent.

44:34.516 --> 44:39.616 align:middle
And I, I don't want to try
to give you specific numbers

44:39.616 --> 44:41.876 align:middle
of things, because that
points to dates, and I,

44:41.876 --> 44:43.046 align:middle
I just don't think-we're
not at a point

44:43.046 --> 44:43.916 align:middle
of being able to do that.

44:46.586 --> 44:47.086 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:49.236 --> 44:49.896 align:middle
Kosuke. KOSUKE TAKAMI.

44:50.106 --> 44:50.936 align:middle
Thank you for doing this.

44:50.936 --> 44:53.096 align:middle
I, I am Kosuke Takami,
with Nikkei.

44:53.586 --> 44:57.656 align:middle
I would like to know about the
impact of a stronger dollar

44:57.876 --> 45:00.176 align:middle
on U.S. economic
growth and prices.

45:00.626 --> 45:04.936 align:middle
And I have no doubt that markets
should determine the exchange

45:04.936 --> 45:10.126 align:middle
rate, but in-Japan and some
emerging markets are suffering

45:10.126 --> 45:11.266 align:middle
from the strong dollar.

45:11.896 --> 45:16.046 align:middle
So I want to know if it is
having a positive impact

45:16.046 --> 45:17.986 align:middle
on U.S. economy in total?

45:18.386 --> 45:18.666 align:middle
Thank you.

45:18.896 --> 45:20.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:20.076 --> 45:23.236 align:middle
So we don't-actually, it's
our-it's our finance ministry,

45:23.236 --> 45:25.296 align:middle
the Treasury Department,
that has a responsibility

45:25.296 --> 45:29.416 align:middle
for thinking about and, and,
if it sees fit, doing things

45:29.416 --> 45:31.036 align:middle
about the level of the dollar.

45:31.586 --> 45:33.926 align:middle
So for us, it's just
another financial variable.

45:33.926 --> 45:36.816 align:middle
And you're right-the dollar
has been-has exhibited,

45:36.816 --> 45:39.616 align:middle
exhibited some strength over the
course of the last year or so

45:39.616 --> 45:41.466 align:middle
because the U.S.
economy is very strong.

45:42.006 --> 45:43.946 align:middle
We don't think of
it as benefiting

45:43.946 --> 45:47.026 align:middle
or hurting the U.S.
We don't-again,

45:47.026 --> 45:49.186 align:middle
we don't manage the
level of the dollar.

45:49.186 --> 45:50.976 align:middle
That's, that's not
our-that's not our job.

45:50.976 --> 45:54.816 align:middle
It's not. You know-and for, for
our economy, what, you know,

45:54.816 --> 45:56.946 align:middle
we're trying to foster
is maximum employment

45:56.946 --> 45:57.816 align:middle
and price stability.

45:58.366 --> 46:00.196 align:middle
And we feel like we've
made good progress on,

46:00.196 --> 46:02.246 align:middle
on both of those goals over
the course of the past year.

46:03.186 --> 46:04.186 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:04.316 --> 46:07.216 align:middle
Jennifer. JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

46:07.786 --> 46:08.606 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

46:08.606 --> 46:10.646 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger,
with Yahoo Finance.

46:10.786 --> 46:14.036 align:middle
You said you would cut
rates before inflation falls

46:14.106 --> 46:14.786 align:middle
to 2 percent.

46:15.146 --> 46:16.856 align:middle
You're forecasting
inflation to end the year

46:16.856 --> 46:18.466 align:middle
at 2.8 percent on core PCE.

46:18.526 --> 46:21.446 align:middle
We're at 2.8 percent,
of course, on core PCE.

46:21.986 --> 46:24.206 align:middle
Right now, you said the
inflation forecast is

46:24.206 --> 46:26.996 align:middle
conservative and that if you
got to 2.6 to 2.7 [percent]

46:26.996 --> 46:29.436 align:middle
on PCE [inflation], that
would be a good place to be.

46:29.806 --> 46:33.066 align:middle
You also indicated the job
market has mostly normalized.

46:33.066 --> 46:37.236 align:middle
So why not cut rates this
summer just once-you're well

46:37.236 --> 46:40.966 align:middle
above neutral-to try to
preserve the soft landing rather

46:40.966 --> 46:43.546 align:middle
than risking waiting
too long in the quest

46:43.546 --> 46:44.656 align:middle
for confidence for inflation?

46:44.656 --> 46:45.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:45.446 --> 46:47.886 align:middle
You know-so we are-we're
well aware

46:47.886 --> 46:49.966 align:middle
of the two-sided risks
here, let me just say.

46:50.026 --> 46:56.146 align:middle
We, we understand that if we
wait too long, that could come

46:56.146 --> 46:58.076 align:middle
at the cost of economic
activity,

46:58.076 --> 46:59.626 align:middle
of employment, of the expansion.

46:59.966 --> 47:03.216 align:middle
We understand that if we move
too, too quickly, we could end

47:03.216 --> 47:05.856 align:middle
up undoing a lot of the good
that we've done and have

47:05.856 --> 47:08.646 align:middle
to then start over, and it
could be very disruptive.

47:08.646 --> 47:12.926 align:middle
So we're, we're extremely aware
of both of those risks and,

47:12.926 --> 47:14.626 align:middle
and just basically
trying to manage them.

47:14.626 --> 47:15.856 align:middle
And what we said is

47:15.856 --> 47:20.436 align:middle
that we'll-that we're-we don't
think it'll be appropriate to,

47:20.436 --> 47:24.056 align:middle
to begin to loosen policy
until we're more confident

47:24.546 --> 47:27.366 align:middle
that inflation is moving
down to 2 percent over time

47:27.436 --> 47:28.496 align:middle
on a sustainable basis.

47:28.496 --> 47:30.406 align:middle
That's-that's kind
of been our test.

47:31.166 --> 47:33.326 align:middle
Or there's another test,
which is, you know,

47:33.326 --> 47:36.326 align:middle
an unexpected deterioration
in labor market conditions.

47:36.326 --> 47:39.116 align:middle
So I think that's,
that's the right way

47:39.116 --> 47:40.176 align:middle
for us to think about it.

47:40.176 --> 47:42.826 align:middle
That's our-that's our
dual mandate there.

47:42.826 --> 47:46.796 align:middle
And, you know, we, we
have a strong economy.

47:46.926 --> 47:51.136 align:middle
We've got growth-all
forecasts are very commonly

47:51.136 --> 47:52.016 align:middle
around 2 percent.

47:52.016 --> 47:54.716 align:middle
That's a-that's a strong
growth rate for our economy.

47:55.046 --> 47:56.926 align:middle
The labor market
continues to print jobs

47:56.926 --> 47:58.276 align:middle
at a pretty high rate.

47:58.846 --> 47:59.906 align:middle
Unemployment is still low.

47:59.906 --> 48:02.666 align:middle
So we have the ability now

48:03.296 --> 48:05.856 align:middle
to approach this question
carefully, and that's,

48:05.916 --> 48:06.496 align:middle
that's what we're doing.

48:06.496 --> 48:06.966 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

48:06.966 --> 48:09.826 align:middle
And just to follow, Chair
Powell-do you need to be ahead

48:10.266 --> 48:11.976 align:middle
of a weakening in
the labor market?

48:11.976 --> 48:13.746 align:middle
Otherwise, is it too late,

48:13.826 --> 48:16.316 align:middle
given that the labor market is
a lagging indicator-right?-when

48:16.316 --> 48:17.356 align:middle
you look at the monthly
jobs data?

48:17.356 --> 48:17.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:17.856 --> 48:20.296 align:middle
Yeah, we, we completely
understand that that's the risk.

48:20.296 --> 48:24.396 align:middle
And that's, that's not what
we're-that's not our plan is

48:24.396 --> 48:26.316 align:middle
to wait for things to break
and then try to fix them.

48:26.896 --> 48:29.426 align:middle
We're, we're trying to balance
these two goals in a way

48:29.426 --> 48:30.936 align:middle
that is consistent
with our framework,

48:31.536 --> 48:32.716 align:middle
and we think we're doing that.

48:34.036 --> 48:35.226 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

48:35.566 --> 48:37.946 align:middle
Evan. EVAN RYSER.

48:38.966 --> 48:39.776 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

48:39.776 --> 48:41.486 align:middle
Evan Ryser with Market
News International.

48:42.386 --> 48:45.566 align:middle
You have mentioned before the
framework review process will

48:45.566 --> 48:47.206 align:middle
start in the latter
half of the year.

48:47.706 --> 48:51.156 align:middle
At this point, do you have
any specific time frame

48:51.156 --> 48:52.256 align:middle
for the start of that?

48:52.456 --> 48:52.836 align:middle
Who will lead it?

48:52.836 --> 48:56.856 align:middle
Will you be-will it be you
or a member-a Committee group

48:57.216 --> 48:58.646 align:middle
of your-of your peers?

48:59.046 --> 49:01.216 align:middle
What will be the parameters?

49:01.456 --> 49:03.886 align:middle
Do you anticipate the
review will consider changes

49:03.886 --> 49:05.186 align:middle
in communications going forward?

49:05.526 --> 49:05.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:05.946 --> 49:08.746 align:middle
So we-what, what
we've been thinking is

49:08.746 --> 49:09.816 align:middle
that we would announce

49:09.956 --> 49:14.036 align:middle
and commence the review
later or late in the year.

49:14.406 --> 49:18.136 align:middle
In the meantime, as
that time approaches,

49:18.136 --> 49:21.216 align:middle
we will be devoting a lot of
careful thought and planning

49:21.216 --> 49:22.826 align:middle
to the contours of it.

49:23.386 --> 49:27.346 align:middle
Within scope would certainly
be communications generally.

49:27.346 --> 49:29.976 align:middle
I-I'm not going to say
that we'll look at this

49:29.976 --> 49:31.026 align:middle
or that particular thing.

49:31.896 --> 49:34.596 align:middle
And all the other details, you
know-we're just-we don't want

49:34.596 --> 49:36.776 align:middle
to get prematurely
into a conversation

49:37.266 --> 49:38.686 align:middle
until it's really
time to have it.

49:38.946 --> 49:40.336 align:middle
And so, you know, I'm going

49:40.336 --> 49:44.246 align:middle
to leave pretty much everything
else to, to later in the year.

49:46.286 --> 49:46.696 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

49:46.696 --> 49:47.966 align:middle
Go to Nancy, for
the last question.

49:48.056 --> 49:49.056 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

49:50.856 --> 49:51.606 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

49:51.606 --> 49:53.956 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer,
with Marketplace.

49:53.956 --> 49:59.516 align:middle
Just one more question on
housing-can you still cut rates

49:59.636 --> 50:02.806 align:middle
with shelter prices
high, or will you wait

50:02.806 --> 50:04.136 align:middle
until they start
to moderate a bit?

50:04.496 --> 50:04.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:06.896 --> 50:11.306 align:middle
I wouldn't-there isn't any
one thing-one variable,

50:11.306 --> 50:15.616 align:middle
like housing prices moderating,
that would really decide

50:15.676 --> 50:17.766 align:middle
or decide against
what we're doing.

50:17.766 --> 50:22.436 align:middle
We've got an overall test,
which is greater confidence

50:22.466 --> 50:24.606 align:middle
that inflation is
moving down to 2 percent

50:24.606 --> 50:25.666 align:middle
on a sustainable basis.

50:25.736 --> 50:27.116 align:middle
That's our overall test.

50:27.116 --> 50:28.466 align:middle
Or, alternatively,

50:28.466 --> 50:31.396 align:middle
we see unexpected weakening
in the labor market.

50:31.826 --> 50:34.226 align:middle
So those are-those are
two different tests.

50:34.226 --> 50:37.516 align:middle
I would say, you know,
we're not-we're not looking

50:37.516 --> 50:39.836 align:middle
at any one price
in any one sector

50:39.836 --> 50:40.866 align:middle
and saying, "That's the one."

50:40.866 --> 50:42.616 align:middle
We don't target housing
prices, for example.

50:43.396 --> 50:45.896 align:middle
And we don't target wages.

50:45.896 --> 50:50.076 align:middle
We, we target aggregate
prices and overall employment,

50:50.076 --> 50:50.726 align:middle
so- NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

50:50.816 --> 50:53.766 align:middle
But if housing prices remain
sticky, could that slow

50:53.766 --> 50:55.366 align:middle
down the pace of rate cuts?

50:55.366 --> 50:55.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:55.726 --> 50:57.466 align:middle
I think you'd look at
the over-we'd be looking

50:57.466 --> 51:00.956 align:middle
at the aggregate numbers
and, and asking ourselves,

51:01.736 --> 51:03.266 align:middle
"What's going on
here with inflation

51:03.416 --> 51:04.716 align:middle
in the-at the aggregate level?"

51:05.096 --> 51:08.836 align:middle
And, you know-and, of
course, if, if-any price

51:08.836 --> 51:11.426 align:middle
that contributed to ongoing
inflation would, would matter.

51:11.426 --> 51:13.006 align:middle
Any price that contributed

51:13.006 --> 51:14.926 align:middle
to ongoing disinflation
would matter, too,

51:15.266 --> 51:16.616 align:middle
but I wouldn't single
out housing

51:16.616 --> 51:18.506 align:middle
as having a, a special
role there.

51:19.596 --> 51:34.176 align:middle
Thank you very much.

